  "query_preview": "..\n\nA rare October severe setup is in place across eastern and\nnorthern portions of the state today as a deep upper level \ntrough and associated jet streak sit over southern California\ninto Arizona. A northward draw of unseasonably moist air along\nwith a strong lower to mid level wind field will produce an \nenvironment conducive to organized supercells with threats of \nvery heavy rainfall rates, damaging wind gusts, large hail, the \npotential for a tornado. \n\nScattered thunderstorms are expected to develop across mainly\neastern areas including eastern Pinal and Graham, Greenlee, and\nCochise (mainly northern Cochise) this afternoon, though this \nthreat may extend in an isolated sense as far west as eastern \nPima or Santa Cruz counties. Instability is more than adequate \ntoday with modeled MLCAPE values of 1000-2000 J/kg, thanks to \nwidespread upper 50s to lower 60s surface dew point and \nafternoon insolation. Additionally, impressively strong deep \nlayer shear on the order of 35-50 knots will provide ample \nsupport for rotating supercells, while low level shear to around\n15 knots in the eastern three counties will provide a tornado \nthreat. In this CAPE/shear environment, discrete supercells will\nlikely produce hail to around 2 inches in diameter and wind \ngusts to 70 mph, along with the threat of a tornado. While the \ntornado threat is non-zero anywhere a storm forms, the \nenvironment in Graham/Greenlee does have a bit of a stronger \nwind field for a tornado, with this field extending south into \nCochise county. This parameter space distribution is covered by \ntoday's Tornado Watch.\n\nWith thunderstorm coverage greatest further north, closer to \nthe upper low and associated jet streak, storm collisions and \nclustering in Graham and Greenlee counties will be likely. Any \nthunderstorm clustering may reduce the hail threat, however the\nflash flooding threat then becomes more concerning. \nPrecipitable water content of 1-1.3 inches combining with the \nlikely intense storms will produce periods of very heavy \nrainfall rates. Storm motions will be fast but any clustering or\ntraining will produce a flash flooding threat.\n\nConvection will exit to the east through this evening, moving\nfully into New Mexico by around 9-11 PM MST. A Tornado Watch is\nin effect until 8 PM with the Flash Flood Watch until 10 PM. \nThe threats will diminish from west to east.\n\nThe upper low will meander east through the region Friday into\nthe weekend. High temperatures will be 5-8 degrees below normal\nacross southeastern Arizona with a welcome drying trend to the\nsurface dew points. Moisture will linger in far eastern areas\nnear the New Mexico border to provide a chance of a shower or\nthunderstorm in the coming afternoons. \n\nOverall forecast temperatures remain below into mid-week next\nweek. Sub-freezing morning lows look likely in the White \nMountains and Hannagan Meadow Sunday and Monday mornings.",
